Planning skill optimization: shell-managed context, deltas, and staleness
==========================================================================

Objective
---------

Reduce token usage during resumed planning sessions by combining:

3. Section-level retrieval.
4. Immutable facts plus delta updates.
5. Hash- and dependency-based freshness checks.

The agent should interact with plan state only through shell commands. It must
not directly edit, append to, or rewrite plan documents or generated context
artifacts. Every write must be performed by a bundled helper that enforces the
canonical formatting contract and writes atomically.

Current problem
---------------

The resume workflow currently requires the agent to read plan progress, the
active goal, goal progress, working context, and completed prerequisite
handoffs. These documents are useful as durable source artifacts, but most of
their contents are unchanged between sessions. Re-reading them in full wastes
context tokens and makes large plans increasingly expensive to resume.

Design principles
-----------------

- Markdown plan documents remain the durable, human-readable source of truth.
- Generated context files are caches and must never silently override source
  documents.
- Stable facts are written once; execution discoveries and decisions are
  recorded as deltas.
- The agent receives only the sections needed for the active work unit.
- Hash checks determine whether generated context is safe to use.
- All generated files use fixed schemas and are created or changed by helpers.
- Helpers write through temporary files followed by an atomic rename.
- Every new format, parser, validator, and mutation path gets bounded tests.

Proposed helper
---------------

Add one bundled command alongside plan-content.sh:

  plan-context.sh <command> [flags]

The helper owns index creation, context-cache creation, delta recording,
section retrieval, processed-set registration, and freshness checks. It should
reuse plan-document-lib.sh for document IDs and section mappings rather than
duplicating document parsing rules.

All commands must use explicit flags. Do not use positional numeric arguments
for plan, goal, step, work-unit, artifact, or paragraph selection. A missing
flag must fail with usage text instead of shifting later arguments into the
wrong field. Existing paragraph-style update flags remain the deliberate
exception because their established syntax is already validated by the
planning helpers.

Required commands
-----------------

1. init

  plan-context.sh init -p 55 -d <plan-directory>

Create the generated context directory and canonical files:

  <plan>/context/index.tsv
  <plan>/context/resume.md
  <plan>/context/facts.tsv
  <plan>/context/deltas.ndjson
  <plan>/context/processed.sha256

The command must refuse to overwrite existing files unless an explicit
rebuild/refresh command is used. It creates index markers but does not place
every source artifact into the processed set.

2. read

  plan-context.sh read -p 55 -g 3 -s 4
  plan-context.sh read -p 55 -w W07
  plan-context.sh read -p 55 -a A12
  plan-context.sh read -p 55 -g 3 -s 4 -I
  plan-context.sh read -p 55 -g 3 -s 4 -C -H

The first form is the primary resume lookup: plan 55, goal 3, step 4. The
helper resolves these numeric selectors through the index and emits compact,
tagged output such as:

  [P55][G03][S04][W07]
  target: A12
  acceptance: ...
  next: ...

Supported selector rules:

- -p/--plan is required and selects a plan by index.
- -g/--goal and -s/--step select nested goal and step indexes.
- -w/--work selects an existing W-style work-unit marker.
- -a/--artifact selects an existing A-style artifact marker.
- -O/--objective selects the objective section.
- -T/--target selects the concrete change-target section.
- -I/--instructions selects the instructions section.
- -C/--acceptance-criteria selects the acceptance-criteria section.
- -H/--handoff selects the handoff section.
- -D/--dependencies selects the dependencies section.
- -R/--risks selects the risks or edge-cases section.
- -V/--verification selects the testing or verification companion.
- -N/--next selects the next-action entry from compact context.
- Section flags are boolean and may be combined. The helper emits sections in
  canonical document order, regardless of flag order.
- -f/--format accepts text, markdown, or json; text is the default compact
  format.
- --full is opt-in and returns the selected flagged sections without expanding
  unrelated sections.

The helper must reject ambiguous combinations, such as a work-unit selector
that does not belong to the selected goal and step. It must not require the
agent to pass a long document ID or section description.

After a successful normal read, the helper registers the resolved source
artifact in the processed set and records its current hash. A --read-only flag
may inspect content without registering it.

3. check

  plan-context.sh check -p 55
  plan-context.sh check -p 55 --changed
  plan-context.sh check -p 55 --all
  plan-context.sh check -p 55 --changed -f json

The default check compares only processed artifacts in
context/processed.sha256. It must not scan or report unread source artifacts.
The --all form is an explicit audit that compares all source artifacts and may
report newly discovered files, but it must not silently add them to the
processed set.

Stable exit codes:

  0  fresh for the selected scope
  1  stale; one or more processed artifacts changed, were added, or removed
  2  invalid; index, manifest, or required context files are missing or bad

The normal stale output must remain compact:

  stale P55
  changed W07 A12
  removed A09
  action: plan-context.sh refresh -p 55 -w W07 -a A12

With --changed, return only the changed markers in a machine-friendly form so
the agent can pass them to refresh without reading long paths. If nothing has
been processed yet, return fresh with a zero exit status even if untouched
source files have changed.

4. refresh

  plan-context.sh refresh -p 55 -w W07 -a A12
  plan-context.sh refresh -p 55 -g 3 -s 4
  plan-context.sh refresh -p 55 --changed

Refresh only the selected processed artifacts. The --changed form obtains the
changed-marker set from check and updates that set; it must not refresh
unprocessed artifacts. A goal/step selector resolves to the processed artifacts
owned by that scope.

Refresh must:

- Re-read only the selected source artifacts.
- Update their hashes in processed.sha256.
- Rebuild only affected compact resume entries.
- Preserve unaffected facts and deltas.
- Write generated files through temporary files and atomic rename.
- Return the changed markers it handled.

5. add-fact

  plan-context.sh add-fact -p 55 -k current-route -v /checkout

Record or replace one confirmed, stable fact in facts.tsv. Keys must be unique,
values must pass the helper's safe-value rules, and duplicate replacement must
require an explicit --replace flag. The agent supplies facts as flags; it never
edits TSV.

6. add-delta

  plan-context.sh add-delta -p 55 -k verification -m 'unit tests passed' -w W07

Append one structured execution event to deltas.ndjson. Supported kinds should
include decision, discovery, verification, blocker, handoff, and next-action.
Each event should contain an ID, timestamp, kind, message, related marker, and
the relevant processed-hash snapshot. The helper must validate the kind and
escape JSON safely.

7. compact

  plan-context.sh compact -p 55

Fold confirmed deltas into facts.tsv or compact resume entries, retain an
auditable compaction marker, and update only affected processed hashes. This is
optional for small plans but prevents unbounded delta growth on long-running
initiatives.

Compaction is a state operation, not an instruction for the current agent to
stop. The helper must exit normally and print a compact continuation marker.
If the host platform performs context compaction that can halt an agent, the
coordinating agent must invoke compact only after the goal worker has returned
its completion result.

Processed hash manifest
-----------------------

Use a generated manifest with one fixed row per processed artifact:

  marker  relative-path  sha256  state

State values should be limited to processed, stale, removed, or superseded.
The manifest must use stable repository-relative paths and must exclude all
generated files under context/ to avoid a hash loop.

Registration rules:

- A successful read registers the resolved A marker and its parent W/G/S
  markers as lookup metadata, but hashes only the source artifact actually
  read.
- A section read hashes the source document containing that section.
- A summary read hashes only the inventory/progress artifacts used to produce
  that summary.
- A read-only request does not register or update hashes.
- Helper mutations to a source document update or invalidate its processed hash
  as part of the same atomic operation, when the affected marker is known.
- Unknown or unread artifacts remain outside the normal stale set.

Resume workflow
---------------

Use this shell-first sequence instead of rereading every plan document:

1. Run:

     plan-context.sh check -p 55

2. If stale, request the compact marker list:

     plan-context.sh check -p 55 --changed

3. Refresh only the reported markers:

     plan-context.sh refresh -p 55 --changed

   Or select a subset explicitly:

     plan-context.sh refresh -p 55 -w W07 -a A12

4. Read compact active context:

     plan-context.sh read -p 55 -g 3 -s 4

5. Retrieve only the sections needed for the next action. Use dedicated flags,
   not a free-form section label:

     plan-context.sh read -p 55 -g 3 -s 4 -I
     plan-context.sh read -p 55 -g 3 -s 4 -C

6. Read a prerequisite handoff only when the W-style dependency list requires
   it, using its marker rather than a long path.

7. Record discoveries, decisions, verification results, blockers, and next
   actions through add-delta. Record durable facts through add-fact.

8. After helper mutations, run check again. Refresh only affected processed
   markers before using cached content.

Goal-worker and coordinator model
----------------------------------

Use one short-lived goal worker per goal when the initiative is large enough
that a single agent context would accumulate unrelated steps, discoveries, and
review output. The coordinator remains responsible for sequencing and does not
carry the full execution history of every goal.

Goal lifecycle:

1. The coordinator reads only the compact plan state and selects the next goal
   by marker, for example `-p 55 -g 3`.
2. The coordinator starts a fresh worker with that goal's marker, work-unit
   list, direct dependencies, and required verification. It does not pass all
   prior goal documents or the entire repository history.
3. The worker uses indexed reads and dedicated section flags, performs the goal,
   records deltas through the helper, and writes the completed handoff through
   the existing plan mutation commands.
4. The worker returns a small completion result containing status, changed
   markers, verification result, blockers, and the next-goal candidate. It does
   not invoke host-level context compaction.
5. The coordinator verifies the worker result, runs the plan validator when
   required, marks the goal complete, and invokes:

     plan-context.sh compact -p 55 -g 3

6. The coordinator then starts the next fresh goal worker. It does not expect
   the compacting worker to continue after compaction.

This avoids the observed failure mode where an agent halts immediately after a
compact operation: compaction belongs to the coordinator's handoff boundary,
not to the worker's active reasoning loop.

Token-cost tradeoff
-------------------

A fresh worker does have startup cost: it must read the compact resume state,
the selected goal, and the minimum prerequisite handoffs. That cost is not
automatically double. It is usually cheaper when the alternative is carrying a
large multi-goal context or repeatedly rereading completed goals. It can cost
more when goals are tiny, highly coupled, or require the same large repository
context to be rediscovered every time.

Use these rules:

- One worker per goal for large or loosely coupled goals.
- Keep one worker across adjacent tiny steps when the goal boundary would add
  more startup context than it removes.
- Always compact at a completed goal boundary when the active context is large,
  even if the same worker continues only when the host platform guarantees that
  compact does not terminate it.
- Prefer the coordinator/worker split when the platform may halt an agent after
  compaction; it makes continuation explicit and reliable.
- Measure representative runs by recording input tokens for startup reads,
  active execution, and handoff. Choose the split only when the saved context
  exceeds the fresh-worker startup cost.

The helper should expose a compact completion summary so the coordinator pays
only for the handoff, not for rereading the worker's full delta history.

No-direct-edit rule
-------------------

The agent-facing planning skill should explicitly say:

- Never use apply_patch, sed -i, perl -i, redirection, or ad-hoc scripts to
  mutate plan files, progress files, context files, inventories, indexes,
  manifests, or reviews.
- Use create-plan.sh, add-goal.sh, add-work-unit.sh, update-plan-content.sh,
  update-step.sh, progress helpers, plan-content.sh, and plan-context.sh.
- Read-only shell commands are allowed for diagnosis, but their output is not a
  substitute for a helper-owned mutation.
- If a required mutation is unsupported, improve the bundled helper and its
  tests before continuing.

Formatting and validation requirements
---------------------------------------

The new helper must follow the maintainer style contract:

- Fixed generated schemas; no decorative or optional columns.
- Stable P/G/S/W/A markers and repository-relative paths.
- Explicit flags for all selectors and values wherever possible.
- Safe shell arguments and JSON escaping.
- Atomic temporary-file plus rename writes.
- No hand-edited paragraph labels or duplicated numbering schemes.
- New mutable Markdown sections must be updated through flagged helper targets.
- New generated formats must be covered by parser, validator, and regression
  tests.

Suggested tests include:

- read -p 55 -g 3 -s 4 resolves the correct indexed artifact.
- Missing selector flags fail without shifting later values.
- read registers only the artifact actually read.
- read --read-only does not change the processed manifest.
- check ignores unread files by default.
- check --all reports unread changes without registering them.
- check returns 0 when the processed set is fresh, 1 after a processed source
  changes, and 2 for malformed or missing context files.
- --changed emits only compact P/G/S/W/A markers.
- refresh --changed updates only those markers.
- generated files are excluded from their own hash scope.
- dedicated flags return only the requested fixed sections, with no -x free-form
  section selector and no short-flag collisions.
- add-fact rejects unsafe or duplicate keys as specified.
- add-delta emits valid escaped NDJSON and preserves prior events.
- refresh and compact writes are atomic and leave no partial artifacts.
- stale context cannot be read until the selected stale markers are refreshed.

Benefits and safeguards
-----------------------

- Indexed flags avoid repeating long descriptions and paths.
- Section retrieval prevents loading unrelated narrative.
- The processed set prevents irrelevant unread changes from causing work.
- Deltas preserve discoveries without rewriting the original plan.
- Selective refresh limits both shell output and model context.
- Hash checks prevent stale cached guidance from being used silently.
- Shell-only mutations provide one formatting and validation boundary.

Hashes detect file changes, not semantic correctness. Material scope changes
must still reopen validation and adversarial review. An explicit --all audit
exists for that case without imposing its cost on every ordinary resume.

Recommended implementation order
---------------------------------

1. Add plan-context.sh with flag parsing and the P/G/S/W/A index.
2. Add read with dedicated, collision-free section flags, processed-set
   registration, check, and selective refresh.
3. Add generated resume.md and processed.sha256 with fixed schemas.
4. Add add-fact and add-delta with facts.tsv and deltas.ndjson.
5. Add compact only after the basic workflow and tests are stable.
6. Update the planning skill's resume section and command reference.
7. Update the maintainer contract and bounded tests for every new format.

The intended steady-state interaction is:

  check -p 55 -> --changed -> refresh selected markers ->
  read -p 55 -g 3 -s 4 -I -> work -> add-delta -> compact at goal boundary
